1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:03,800
If you're using PLS-SEM for your thesis,

2
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:05,415
there's a good chance you're making

3
00:00:05,445 --> 00:00:07,400
at least one of these seven mistakes.

4
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:09,350
And I'm not guessing.

5
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:11,876
Every point in this video comes straight

6
00:00:11,906 --> 00:00:15,510
from one of the most widely used
references on PLS-SEM:

7
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:19,110
A Primer on Partial Least Squares
Structural Equation Modeling,

8
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:23,390
third edition, by Hair, Hult, Ringle, and
Sarstedt.

9
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:25,109
Let's get into it.

10
00:00:25,793 --> 00:00:27,123
Mistake number one:

11
00:00:27,153 --> 00:00:28,701
running a simultaneous F-test.

12
00:00:28,731 --> 00:00:31,643
The F-test belongs to multiple regression.

13
00:00:31,673 --> 00:00:33,577
In chapter six, Hair lays out

14
00:00:33,607 --> 00:00:35,667
how to evaluate a structural model:

15
00:00:35,697 --> 00:00:38,582
check collinearity, test the path
coefficients,

16
00:00:38,612 --> 00:00:41,763
assess explanatory power, and assess
predictive power.

17
00:00:41,793 --> 00:00:44,142
There's no F-test on that list.

18
00:00:44,172 --> 00:00:46,182
If you want the combined effect of your
predictors,

19
00:00:46,212 --> 00:00:48,202
look at R squared.

20
00:00:48,232 --> 00:00:51,265
Hair describes it as the exogenous
variables'

21
00:00:51,295 --> 00:00:54,082
combined effects on the endogenous
variable.

22
00:00:54,112 --> 00:00:55,882
So instead of an F-test,

23
00:00:55,912 --> 00:01:00,270
report R squared together with the
significance of each path.

24
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:02,294
Mistake number two:

25
00:01:02,324 --> 00:01:04,410
reporting a goodness-of-fit index.

26
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,746
The GoF was proposed early on

27
00:01:07,776 --> 00:01:10,470
as a way to validate PLS models globally.

28
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:12,387
But research showed it can't separate

29
00:01:12,417 --> 00:01:14,232
valid models from invalid ones.

30
00:01:14,262 --> 00:01:15,670
Hair's advice is direct:

31
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:20,062
researchers are advised not to use this
measure in PLS-SEM.

32
00:01:20,092 --> 00:01:22,042
And the other proposed fit measures?

33
00:01:22,072 --> 00:01:23,562
In their current form,

34
00:01:23,592 --> 00:01:26,222
they offer little value for applied
research.

35
00:01:26,252 --> 00:01:31,226
Focus on R squared, effect sizes, and
predictive power instead.

36
00:01:31,914 --> 00:01:33,315
Mistake number three:

37
00:01:33,345 --> 00:01:36,384
choosing PLS-SEM just because your sample
is small.

38
00:01:36,414 --> 00:01:40,519
Hair calls this probably the most often
misused argument

39
00:01:40,549 --> 00:01:41,884
for using PLS-SEM.

40
00:01:41,914 --> 00:01:44,164
No method has magic capabilities.

41
00:01:44,194 --> 00:01:47,613
The popular ten-times rule is only a rough
guideline.

42
00:01:47,643 --> 00:01:50,353
Think in terms of statistical power
instead.

43
00:01:50,383 --> 00:01:52,253
With the inverse square root method,

44
00:01:52,283 --> 00:01:55,073
if your weakest expected path is about
0.2,

45
00:01:55,103 --> 00:01:58,713
you need around 155 observations

46
00:01:58,743 --> 00:02:00,833
at a 5% significance level.

47
00:02:00,863 --> 00:02:02,562
And if you ran a pilot study,

48
00:02:02,592 --> 00:02:06,531
use its smallest path coefficient as your
starting point.

49
00:02:07,208 --> 00:02:08,518
Mistake number four:

50
00:02:08,548 --> 00:02:11,018
relying on the Fornell-Larcker criterion.

51
00:02:11,048 --> 00:02:13,343
For decades, it was the go-to test

52
00:02:13,373 --> 00:02:14,898
for discriminant validity.

53
00:02:14,928 --> 00:02:17,286
But Hair notes it performs very poorly

54
00:02:17,316 --> 00:02:19,618
when indicator loadings are similar,

55
00:02:19,648 --> 00:02:22,337
which is usually the case with real data.

56
00:02:22,367 --> 00:02:24,817
Use the HTMT instead:

57
00:02:24,847 --> 00:02:28,757
below 0.90, or below 0.85

58
00:02:28,787 --> 00:02:31,217
when your constructs are conceptually
distinct.

59
00:02:31,247 --> 00:02:34,957
In other words, an HTMT above 0.90

60
00:02:34,987 --> 00:02:37,869
is a red flag for discriminant validity.

61
00:02:38,588 --> 00:02:40,198
Mistake number five:

62
00:02:40,228 --> 00:02:42,578
testing mediation with the Sobel test.

63
00:02:42,608 --> 00:02:44,058
Hair is blunt here:

64
00:02:44,088 --> 00:02:46,778
the Sobel test should no longer be used.

65
00:02:46,808 --> 00:02:49,318
It assumes a normal distribution,

66
00:02:49,348 --> 00:02:52,835
which doesn't fit a nonparametric method
like PLS-SEM.

67
00:02:52,865 --> 00:02:55,794
Bootstrap the indirect effect instead.

68
00:02:55,824 --> 00:02:58,854
Bootstrapping makes no distributional
assumptions,

69
00:02:58,884 --> 00:03:02,505
which is exactly why it suits PLS-SEM.

70
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:04,980
Mistake number six:

71
00:03:05,010 --> 00:03:08,060
celebrating a reliability of 0.97.

72
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:12,140
Values between 0.70 and 0.90 are
satisfactory.

73
00:03:12,170 --> 00:03:15,040
But values above 0.90,

74
00:03:15,070 --> 00:03:18,920
and definitely above 0.95, are not
desirable.

75
00:03:18,950 --> 00:03:21,320
They usually mean your items are
redundant,

76
00:03:21,350 --> 00:03:24,390
slightly rephrasing the very same
question.

77
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:26,830
So if your reliability looks too perfect,

78
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:30,401
check whether two items are asking the
same thing.

79
00:03:31,123 --> 00:03:32,713
Mistake number seven:

80
00:03:32,743 --> 00:03:35,633
judging R squared with one universal
cutoff.

81
00:03:35,663 --> 00:03:38,107
Hair says acceptable R squared values

82
00:03:38,137 --> 00:03:39,713
are based on the context.

83
00:03:39,743 --> 00:03:42,988
In some disciplines, 0.10 is satisfactory.

84
00:03:43,018 --> 00:03:47,213
In others, researchers expect values above
0.65.

85
00:03:47,243 --> 00:03:47,984
Also remember:

86
00:03:48,014 --> 00:03:52,052
the more predictors you add, the higher R
squared tends to get.

87
00:03:52,082 --> 00:03:54,852
So compare your R squared with similar
studies,

88
00:03:54,882 --> 00:03:57,077
not with a magic number.

89
00:03:57,683 --> 00:03:58,573
Quick recap.

90
00:03:58,603 --> 00:04:00,364
No F-test. No GoF.

91
00:04:00,394 --> 00:04:02,053
No small-sample excuse.

92
00:04:02,083 --> 00:04:04,373
HTMT over Fornell-Larcker.

93
00:04:04,403 --> 00:04:05,873
Bootstrapping over Sobel.

94
00:04:05,903 --> 00:04:08,453
Watch out for reliability that's too high.

95
00:04:08,483 --> 00:04:11,353
And read R squared in context.

96
00:04:11,383 --> 00:04:14,093
If you want all of this in one practical
guide,

97
00:04:14,123 --> 00:04:17,226
check out the SEM-PLS guide in the
description.

98
00:04:17,256 --> 00:04:19,422
And subscribe to Not Your Lecture

99
00:04:19,452 --> 00:04:23,111
for more research tips that actually hold
up.

